why 1 3/4 MELODIC The bass leaps down a diminished seventh, A♭ to B, then steps up to C. An expressive leap in the St John Passion, settled by the step after it.
why 5 0 RANGE The octave leap lifts the bass to a high E♭ to keep the chord close under the climbing tune; it walks back down D–C–B♭–A at once.
why 5 1/8 RANGE Still high: the bass is stepping down from E♭ and is back in its usual range two beats later.
why 6 1/8 DOUBLING Over the bass's held B♭ the upper voices make a neighbor figure, D–C–D, B♭–A–B♭ and F–E♭–F. A passing sound over a held bass, so the ⁶₄ doubling rule does not apply.
why 6 1/2 DIRECT The tune leaps D–B♭ as the bass drops an octave, both within one B♭ chord. Nothing new is hidden, since the chord stays the same.
why 7 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tune falls D–C–B♭ to the half cadence, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The music is heading for B♭ (V), where D is not a leading tone.
why 9 3/4 CROSS_RELATION Tenor and bass move chromatically in opposite directions, E♭–D♭ and D–E, into a diminished seventh chord that leads to F minor. Two chromatic lines crossing like this give the passage its sharp pain.
why 10 0 TENDENCY The tune holds B♭ into the F minor chord and then falls by step, A♭–G, to the fermata. It does fall, a beat later, as the melody wants.
